// A recipe, written from md-docs/recipes/keyboard-and-screen-reader.md by scripts/docsite/recipes.py;
// edit the recipe, not this file.
page RecipeKeyboardAndScreenReader(path: "/recipes/keyboard-and-screen-reader", title: "Make a page work with a keyboard and a screen reader — Recipes", description: "Name every control, make a custom control work from the keyboard, then check the page with a keyboard, a screen reader, zoom and reduced motion.") {
    RecipeShell("Make a page work with a keyboard and a screen reader", summary: "The built-ins already render the markup assistive technology expects, and the compiler reports what it can see in that markup ([Accessibility](/about/accessibility)). What is left is yours: a name for every control that matches what a sighted reader sees, and a keyboard path through any element you turned into a control. This page has a labelled search field, an icon-only button, and a card that acts as a button:", outputs: ["website", "app"], since: "", recipes: RECIPE_INDEX, sections: [Anchor(id: "how-it-works", label: "How it works"), Anchor(id: "check-it-with-assistive-technology", label: "Check it with assistive technology"), Anchor(id: "a-checklist", label: "A checklist")], related: [RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Test a page", to: "/recipes/test-a-page"), RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Keep the active tab in the address", to: "/recipes/active-tab-in-the-address"), RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Keep filters in the address", to: "/recipes/filters-in-the-address")], source: "md-docs/recipes/keyboard-and-screen-reader.md", how: true, hasVariations: false, project: { "src/pages/KeyboardAndScreenReader.wf": #"page Plans(path: "/plans", title: "Plans", description: "Choose a plan.") {
    state q = ""
    state chosen = ""
    Heading("Plans").h1
    Input(bind: q, label: "Search the plans", hint: "Try “team” or “solo”").search
    IconButton(icon: "close", label: "Clear the search") { on click { q = "" } }
    Button("Search", aria-label: "Search the plans") { on click { log(q) } }
    Card(role: "button", tabindex: "0") {
        on click { chosen = "Team" }
        on key("Enter") { chosen = "Team" }
        on key("space") { chosen = "Team" }
        Text("Team plan")
    }
    Text("Chosen: {chosen}")
}
"#, "webfluent.app.json": #"{
  "name": "keyboard-and-screen-reader"
}
"# }) {
        code {
            CodeBlock(#"page Plans(path: "/plans", title: "Plans", description: "Choose a plan.") {
    state q = ""
    state chosen = ""
    Heading("Plans").h1
    Input(bind: q, label: "Search the plans", hint: "Try “team” or “solo”").search
    IconButton(icon: "close", label: "Clear the search") { on click { q = "" } }
    Button("Search", aria-label: "Search the plans") { on click { log(q) } }
    Card(role: "button", tabindex: "0") {
        on click { chosen = "Team" }
        on key("Enter") { chosen = "Team" }
        on key("space") { chosen = "Team" }
        Text("Team plan")
    }
    Text("Chosen: {chosen}")
}"#, title: "wf")
        }
        Markdown("- **A visible `label:`, not a placeholder.** `label:` is a real `<label for>`, and `hint:` is tied to the control with `aria-describedby`. A placeholder disappears as soon as the reader types, so prefer a label; `A03` warns of an `Input` with neither.\n- **An icon-only control has a name.** `label:` on an `IconButton`, or `aria-label:` on anything else, is read aloud and never shown (`A02`).\n- **An `aria-label` begins with the visible words.** Someone who says \"click Search\" to voice control then reaches the right button. One that does not contain them is an `A15`: a `Button(\"Save\", aria-label: \"Submit the form\")` should say `aria-label: \"Save the note\"`.\n- **A custom control needs a role, a place in the tab order, and keys.** Use `Button` whenever you can. When an element must act as one, as the `Card` here does, give it `role: \"button\"`, `tabindex: \"0\"` and handlers for the keys a button answers to, Enter and Space. Without `tabindex`, the card cannot be reached with Tab; without the key handlers, reaching it does nothing.")

        Heading("Check it with assistive technology", id: "check-it-with-assistive-technology").h2

        Markdown("The compiler's checks are a floor. Before you ship a page, use it the way its readers do:\n\n1. **Use only the keyboard.** Put the mouse away. Tab through the page, open and close every menu and dialog, and submit every form. Focus is always visible and never lost.\n2. **Use a screen reader.** VoiceOver (macOS: ⌘F5), NVDA (Windows, free) or TalkBack (Android). Listen to the page's list of headings and landmarks, and to what a form says when it fails.\n3. **Zoom to 200%**, and narrow the window. Nothing needs horizontal scrolling.\n4. **Turn on reduced motion** in your system settings, and reload. Nothing moves.\n\nPart of this can be automated. `wf test`'s `click \"Save\"` and `type \"…\" into \"Email\"` find controls by the name a reader sees, the way assistive technology does, so a test that passes is a page someone can operate ([Test a page](/recipes/test-a-page)).")

        Heading("A checklist", id: "a-checklist").h2

        Markdown("- `wf check` reports no `A` warnings.\n- Every picture's alt text says what it shows, and decorative ones have `alt: \"\"`.\n- Every link and button says what it does without the text around it.\n- Every control has a visible label, and every `aria-label` contains the visible words.\n- Colour is never the only way something is said.\n- Every custom control is reachable with Tab and works with Enter and Space.\n- The page works with only a keyboard, with a screen reader, at 200% zoom, and with reduced motion.")

    }
}
